# What explore mode actually does, and why it is refused for a human-versus-
# human game.
#
# The mode's distinguishing behaviour is that it answers every human move with
# the *kibitz* engine — and it consults currentKibitz(), never the colour
# assignment. So entering it with two humans would quietly turn the game into
# human-versus-engine, which is what setGameMode() refuses. It used to refuse
# silently: the menu entry simply failed to light up.
#
# Kibitz on demand is a separate thing and needs no mode change; the Kibitz
# button works in an ordinary match. That is what makes the refusal acceptable
# rather than a missing feature. See docs/game-modes.md.

# --- 1. Refused for human versus human --------------------------------------
new_game 9
wait_idle
switch_player black human
switch_player white human
wait_idle
expect mode match

toggle_explore_mode
wait_idle
expect mode match          # unchanged, and the refusal now says why

# --- 2. Kibitz does not need explore mode ----------------------------------
# Deliberately issued straight after a move, with no settling delay. Between two
# moves nobody is blocked in genmove — the loop clears playerToMove and sleeps
# 500 ms — and a kibitz request arriving in that window used to be dropped, so
# this needed a `wait 900` to pass. It now queues, exactly as a local move does.
pass
wait_until move_count >= 1
wait_idle
expect phase playing

play once
wait_until move_count >= 2
wait_idle
# The kibitz engine played a move in a game whose two players are both human.
expect move_count 2
expect mode match

# --- 3. With an engine on the board, explore mode is available -------------
switch_player white mock
wait_idle
toggle_explore_mode
wait_idle
expect mode explore

# --- 4. In explore mode the reply ignores the colour assignment ------------
# Loading a game that carries a result selects explore mode by itself.
load_sgf tests/data/resign.sgf
wait_idle
switch_player black human
switch_player white human
wait_idle
expect mode explore
expect main_line_moves 3

navigate_back
wait_idle
expect view_position 2

# One variation from the human. Both players are human, yet the position
# advances by *two* moves: the kibitz engine answers regardless.
click 0 0
wait_idle
expect holds_stone true
click 0 0
wait_until view_position >= 4
wait_idle
expect view_position 4
expect black_stones 2
expect white_stones 2
